VeridianOS: complete QVideoFrame in QPlatformVideoSource's meta-object code

GCC 16 reported, in Multimedia's mocs_compilation.cpp: "qvideoframe.h:26:
defining 'QVideoFrame', which previously failed to be complete in a SFINAE
context [-Wsfinae-incomplete]". platform/qplatformvideosource_p.h only
forward-declares QVideoFrame but declares the signal
newVideoFrame(const QVideoFrame &); its moc code, included in
mocs_compilation.cpp before moc_qvideoframe.cpp, asks QMetaType whether
QVideoFrame is complete, gets "no", and qvideoframe.h then defines it in
the same translation unit. The meta-type answer must not differ within a
program, so this is a real (if benign so far) inconsistency.

Q_MOC_INCLUDE(<QtMultimedia/qvideoframe.h>) makes moc include the full
definition in the generated code, which is the documented mechanism for
forward-declared signal argument types and is already used for
qvideoframe.h in multimediaquick/qquickvideooutput_p.h. The header itself
still only forward-declares the class; the compiler ignores
Q_MOC_INCLUDE. No behaviour change.


diff -ru a/src/multimedia/platform/qplatformvideosource_p.h b/src/multimedia/platform/qplatformvideosource_p.h
--- a/src/multimedia/platform/qplatformvideosource_p.h	2026-10-08 17:34:50.382615903 -0400
+++ b/src/multimedia/platform/qplatformvideosource_p.h	2026-10-08 17:35:11.008767437 -0400
@@ -30,6 +30,9 @@
 class Q_MULTIMEDIA_EXPORT QPlatformVideoSource : public QObject
 {
     Q_OBJECT
+    // QVideoFrame is only forward-declared here; the meta-object code for
+    // newVideoFrame() needs the complete type.
+    Q_MOC_INCLUDE(<QtMultimedia/qvideoframe.h>)
 public:
     using QObject::QObject;
 
